# Line endings for this repository.
#
# REGIME: text files are stored with LF in the object database and checked out
# with CRLF in the working tree. This repo is Windows-first (PowerShell scripts,
# editors that default to CRLF, a CRLF-heavy history), so CRLF is the friendly
# checkout format — while LF in the blobs keeps diffs, patches and the Linux CI
# leg stable and makes the repository independent of any contributor's
# `core.autocrlf` setting.
#
# Why this file exists: 6 library files were committed with CRLF blobs while the
# other 85 tracked files held LF, so every edit to those 6 turned into a
# whole-file diff under `core.autocrlf=true` (and a whole-file EOL flip under
# `false`). Declaring the rule once removes that class of noise for good: with
# `eol=crlf`, git normalises CRLF→LF when staging (so a writer that emits either
# style stays clean) and materialises CRLF on checkout.
#
# Staging an edit: plain `git add` is fine — no `-c core.autocrlf=…` needed.
#
# Catch-all first, specific rules after: for a given attribute the LAST matching
# line wins, so `* text=auto` must come before the extension rules to let them
# override it. `text=auto` also keeps real binaries (none today) unconverted.

* text=auto

# Source, config, docs and scripts: normalise to LF in the repository, CRLF on
# checkout.
*.js    text eol=crlf
*.cjs   text eol=crlf
*.mjs   text eol=crlf
*.ts    text eol=crlf
*.json  text eol=crlf
*.md    text eol=crlf
*.yml   text eol=crlf
*.yaml  text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf
*.txt   text eol=crlf

# Extensionless dotfiles that must follow the same rule.
.gitattributes text eol=crlf
.gitignore     text eol=crlf
.zcodeignore   text eol=crlf

# Generated bundles: LF on checkout, NOT crlf.
#
# These two are build output committed for distribution, and
# `scripts/build-client.mjs --check` compares the file on disk byte for byte
# against what esbuild writes (esbuild always emits LF). Forcing CRLF on checkout
# therefore made the check fail on EVERY fresh clone — which is exactly what CI
# does — while a developer's stale LF working copy kept passing locally. Pinning
# them to LF keeps disk state equal to build output on every platform. (The check
# also normalises CRLF before comparing now, so an editor that rewrites them
# cannot fail the gate for a line-ending reason either.) These lines come after
# the `*.js` rule on purpose: the last matching line wins.
lib/client.js        text eol=lf
lib/client.panels.js text eol=lf

# Binary assets, should any be added: never normalise, never diff as text.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf  binary
*.zip  binary
*.gz   binary
